{
  "status": "ok",
  "data": {
    "query": "US soybean exports China farm belt retaliation 2018 2019 Trump tariff impact agriculture",
    "search_window": {
      "from": "2018-07-13",
      "to": "2019-01-09"
    },
    "sources": {
      "grounding": {
        "generic": [
          {
            "url": "https://www.politico.com/story/2018/10/23/trump-tariffs-farmers-agriculture-866450",
            "title": "Trump tariffs lead to bleak 2019 farm forecasts - POLITICO",
            "snippets": [
              "# Trump tariffs lead to bleak 2019 farm forecasts\nGordon, a member of the American Soybean Association Board of Directors, estimated fertilizer costs in spring 2019 will be 15 percent more than this year for him. Fertilizer prices have jumped since spring, due in part to lower production levels in China.\n\n“We have one of the lowest years of farmer profitability, and yet next year our inputs are going to be higher,” he said.",
              "The biggest factor taking aim at farm incomes has to do with supply and demand economics. Commodity prices have plummeted since spring, when China was gearing up to slap 25 percent duties on U.S. agricultural goods like soybeans in retaliation for Trump’s tariffs on Chinese high-tech products",
              "Soybeans are the poster crop for the current trade pain, with prices down roughly $2 per bushel, or 20 percent. U.S. growers have effectively been shut out of the world’s largest soy market in China since those retaliatory tariffs went into effect in July.\n\n“Prices have just fallen off a cliff since those tariffs went into effect,” said Grant Kimberley, a corn and soybean farmer in central Iowa and director of market development at the Iowa Soybean Association",
              "USDA’s Foreign Agricultural Service also estimates that “a large pullback in Chinese demand for U.S. soybeans appears likely to continue well into 2018-2019.” Record soybean production and large ending stocks projected by USDA could further push down crop prices going into next year.\n\nWidmar, the agricultural economist, said resolving the trade spat with China would produce a “much more positive outlook for 2019.”",
              "Exacerbating the trade pain is a crop storage logjam in parts of the country stretching from North Dakota to Louisiana.\n\nAfter soybean sales to China dried up, the commodity began piling up in grain elevators or rerouted to different seaports like New Orleans to export to other markets including South America, Europe and Africa. The backlog has left little room for newly gathered crops and widened the gap between market commodity prices and the cash payments farmers receive for their crops at the elevator"
            ]
          },
          {
            "url": "https://www.latimes.com/business/la-fi-soybean-farmers-tariffs-20180816-story.html",
            "title": "In soybean country, Trump's trade war with China tests patience ...",
            "snippets": [
              "Soybean farmers in Nebraska so far are giving Trump time to win his trade war against China, but that could change once they really start to feel the pain of his policies.\nBusiness",
              "# In soybean country, Trump’s trade war with China tests patience and nerves\nBut that streak is in jeopardy, thanks to President Trump’s trade war with China, which has pummeled soybean exports and prices. Increasingly anxious about his family’s livelihood, the 44-year-old has taken small steps to save money, such as canceling satellite television and buying a used combine harvester rather than a new one",
              "One thing hasn’t changed: his support for Trump, whom Saathoff credits for taking China to task for its unfair trading behavior. He and some of his fellow farmers are willing to give the president more time to win concessions, betting that Trump won’t let things get really bad for American soybean farmers, who export about one-fourth of their crops to the Chinese market. But he also warned that their patience won’t last forever.",
              "“He’s probably the only president who knew soybean farmers exist,” said the soft-spoken Saathoff. “I don’t know if we’re ready to rebel or not. But let’s just get the trade policy done.”\n\nSaathoff can wait it out for at least a few more months partly because he was able to sell some of his fall harvest in advance, before soybean prices dropped. The question is: Will he and other farmers stick with Trump when they really start to feel the pain?",
              "Even so, Trump’s trade policies have sparked tensions in farm communities, in some cases dividing fathers and sons. And many farmers are getting restless as harvest season nears. Sen. Ben Sasse (R-Neb.) has been a frequent critic of Trump’s trade actions, calling some of his moves “dumb” and saying Trump is “threatening to light American agriculture on fire.”",
              "Few things are as important to America’s heartland economy as soybeans, whose growers stand to be among the biggest losers in a protracted trade fight with China. U.S. farmers shipped about $14 billion in soybeans to China in 2016, up from $1 billion in 2001. That makes soybeans one of the two top U.S. exports to China — the other is aircraft and parts",
              "As rising incomes in China have boosted that nation’s appetite for meat, the Chinese mostly use American soybeans as feed for livestock.\n\nAfter Trump slapped tariffs on Chinese products this summer, Beijing fired back with retaliatory levies on U.S. soybeans and many other farm goods. In recent weeks, China has essentially halted new purchases of soybeans while moving to shift orders to other countries and providing incentives to its domestic farmers to produce more beans.",
              "One result has been a drop in soybean prices, from about $11 a bushel earlier in the year to less than $9 today — the break-even point for many farmers",
              "Their worst fear is a permanent loss of the China business. Some of the older farmers remember what happened after Washington imposed a broad embargo of grain exports in 1973. Japan, worried about a stable supply of soybeans, reacted by making big investments in Brazil’s then-small soybean industry. Today, Brazil and the United States are the world’s two largest exporters of soybeans",
              "In speeches, Trump has called for patience, and has sought to soften the blow to farmers by offering $12 billion in emergency relief. Farmers here and throughout the country largely dismissed Trump’s offer as a handout that wouldn’t go very far, politically or financially."
            ]
          },
          {
            "url": "https://www.everycrsreport.com/reports/R45448.html",
            "title": "Profiles and Effects of Retaliatory Tariffs on U.S. Agricultural ...",
            "snippets": [
              "# Profiles and Effects of Retaliatory Tariffs on U.S. Agricultural Exports\nChina, which is subject to both the U.S. Section 232 steel and aluminum tariffs and separate Section 301 tariffs aimed as punishment for its handling of U.S. intellectual property rights, has placed retaliatory tariffs on the largest number and highest value of U.S. agricultural and food products. More than 800 products—including soybeans, pork, dairy products, fruits and nuts, seafood, and processed products—accounting for almost all U.S",
              "agricultural and food exports to China in value terms in 2017 (the last full year without retaliatory tariffs) are now subject to additional tariffs of 5%, 10%, 15%, 25%, or a combination of those amounts. U.S. exports to China of those products that are subject to retaliatory tariffs were worth about $20.6 billion in 2017. China has fallen from the leading export market for U.S",
              "## Summary\nCommodities that are highly dependent on exports to the retaliating markets may be more severely affected than others by any loss of markets from the tariffs. For instance, commodities for which U.S. exports to the retaliating countries represent 30% or more of its total exports include soybeans, sorghum, pork, cheese, apples, cherries, seafood, ginseng, whiskey, and some processed foods.",
              "soybean exports to China for January through October 2018 are 63% lower than during the same time period in 2017—a change due in large part to the tariffs",
              "These retaliatory tariffs may adversely impact U.S. farmers, ranchers, and food producers by eroding the competitiveness of U.S. commodities in these important markets. Mexico, Canada, China, and the EU are among the top five export markets for agricultural products in value terms. Increased tariffs on U.S. exports to those markets—even for a short period of time—could mean reduced sales for U.S. farmers, ranchers, and food producers.",
              "## Overview of Retaliatory Tariffs on U.S. Agricultural and Food Products\n### Potential Effects on U.S Agricultural Exports\nUSDA forecasts a decrease in the value of total U.S. agricultural exports for FY2019 of $1.9 billion from FY2018 exports of $143.4 billion.^10 The forecast decrease compared with FY2018 reflects mainly reduced soybean exports due to the retaliatory tariffs and reduced cotton exports due to slowing global demand",
              "Additionally, the authors suggest that soybean producers will see the most change in the wake of tariff retaliation, with exports potentially falling by 21% and land prices declining by about 18%.^12",
              "### The Targeting of Agricultural and Food Products\nFurther, many of the targeted agricultural and food products are produced in other exporting countries, giving importing countries alternative sources for those products. For example, while the United States is a leading soybean exporter, Brazil, Argentina, and Paraguay also produce significant exportable quantities of that crop. Thus, China can turn to these alternative suppliers for soybeans",
